---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="About">
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-b from-orange-50 to-orange-100 font-jakarta text-gray-800 px-4 py-20 flex justify-center">
    <div class="max-w-4xl w-full">
      <h1 class="text-6xl font-extrabold tracking-tight mb-6 text-center text-gray-900">About BABLR</h1>
      <h2 class=" text-center text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-500 to-pink-500 mb-12 pb-1">
        The program that wants to write itself.
      </h2>
      <div class="bg-white/30 backdrop-blur-lg border border-white/20 shadow-xl rounded-3xl p-8 md:p-16 text-center">
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed md:text-xl text-gray-700 mb-8">
          In 2020, The Inventor was intrigued with the idea that embedding rich information in text could be far more powerful.
          If a program knows everything about itself, it can guide you in the process of writing it.
          Why not let programs lead us to what they want—rather than relying on a scattered toolkit that isn’t up to the task?
        </p>
        <blockquote class="italic text-xl text-orange-800 border-l-4 border-orange-400 pl-4">
          “Programs that know themselves can shape themselves.”
          <br />
          <span class="block text-sm text-orange-600 mt-2">— The Inventor</span>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>